1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:03,761
This video will talk about logging and
the syslog utility.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:10,455
So you're familiar with, at this point,
printf debugging, probably use that for

3
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:12,980
all your C development that you've done so
far.

4
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,656
And you know that print output is one of
the simplest ways that you can use to

5
00:00:16,656 --> 00:00:18,862
troubleshoot and debug your application.

6
00:00:18,862 --> 00:00:22,034
And it really works great for
interactive programs, but

7
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:25,614
what about when you're working
on a headless embedded system?

8
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:28,088
What can you do for
using the same kind of,

9
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:33,240
what's a simple way that you could
troubleshoot and debug in that case?

10
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,387
So one of the things that you should think
about in a headless embedded system is

11
00:00:36,387 --> 00:00:37,540
how to do logging.

12
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:41,804
You need typically some way to store
information in log files that you could go

13
00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:43,116
in and retrieve later,

14
00:00:43,116 --> 00:00:47,839
given that you don't have this interactive
component of running an application like

15
00:00:47,839 --> 00:00:51,004
you would if you were just
writing a standalone program.

16
00:00:51,004 --> 00:00:54,925
And there's several different
logging frameworks out there, but

17
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:56,231
one of the simplest and

18
00:00:56,231 --> 00:01:01,061
the one that we're going to cover in this
course is syslog and syslogd, or rsyslogd.

19
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:07,208
So this is really a daemon, which we'll
talk about more later in the course,

20
00:01:07,208 --> 00:01:11,010
but it's basically an application
that runs in the background.

21
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:15,942
It uses configuration to configure logging
that is written to the file system, and

22
00:01:15,942 --> 00:01:21,240
typically you'll find those log files
written in the var/log directory.

23
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,219
And there's a set of API calls for

24
00:01:24,219 --> 00:01:29,341
syslog that will automatically
write the log messages.

25
00:01:29,341 --> 00:01:33,261
You send two files in this directory
based on some rules that you set up.

26
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:36,792
So if you do man syslog,

27
00:01:36,792 --> 00:01:40,787
you can see more information about
the usage of this utility, but

28
00:01:40,787 --> 00:01:45,750
it's really about splitting your log
message into facilities and priorities.

29
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:50,429
And so with your facilities,
you can say this is a user application so

30
00:01:50,429 --> 00:01:54,080
this is maybe something
that would run interactive.

31
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,938
This is a daemon application
meaning something that's running in

32
00:01:56,938 --> 00:01:57,720
the background.

33
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:02,707
Or you can have these LOCAL0 to LOCAL7,
just your own defined facility

34
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:07,461
that you use for your application to
split up the type of log message.

35
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:12,008
And then there's also a priority
associated with the log message, so

36
00:02:12,008 --> 00:02:14,301
you can say there's an error
warning info or debug.

37
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:18,781
And then you can set different rules based
on the priority to say maybe you don't log

38
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:19,370
it at all.

39
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:21,730
Maybe you log it to a specific location.

40
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:26,322
Maybe you display it on the terminal,
if it's an error message for

41
00:02:26,322 --> 00:02:29,766
instance, rather than just
logging it to a file.

42
00:02:29,766 --> 00:02:32,440
The usage of syslog is really simple.

43
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:36,643
It's just you open log with the facility
that I mentioned earlier and

44
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:41,570
then any time you want to use it, you just
use syslog, specify the logging level,

45
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:45,540
and then just use exactly
like you would with printf.

46
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:49,847
And so for this simple example that I'm
showing here results in a message like

47
00:02:49,847 --> 00:02:54,310
this in /var/log/syslog where we're
printing out the print if statement.

48
00:02:54,310 --> 00:02:57,661
But rather than going to the terminal,
it's going to go to this log file.

49
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,423
So in terms of how syslog is configured,

50
00:03:01,423 --> 00:03:03,941
it will be a little
different on every system.

51
00:03:03,941 --> 00:03:10,669
But for the Ubuntu application or Ubuntu
OS or virtual machine that we're using for

52
00:03:10,669 --> 00:03:15,860
this course, it's going to happen
based on rules that are located

53
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:21,451
in /etc/rsyslog.d, and
then just some message default.conf.

54
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:26,740
So what we can see here
is that the facility and

55
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:31,241
log level if it's not
mentioned here specifically.

56
00:03:31,241 --> 00:03:34,221
So like the log user that we mentioned,
because it's not mentioned

57
00:03:34,221 --> 00:03:37,951
here specifically, it's going to end up
getting written into /var/log/syslog.